\n</memory>\n\n<reflection>\nThe last action involved performing another Google search to find gender-specific data on tertiary education from the 2011 Bulgarian census. The search yielded useful information regarding the number of women (791.8 thousand) who completed tertiary education. However, the number of men was not found in the results. Although partial progress was made, the task goal remains incomplete as both gender counts are necessary to determine the requested difference.\n</reflection>\n\n<plan>\nThe next step is to search specifically for the number of men who completed tertiary education according to the 2011 Bulgarian census. Knowing the exact number of men, alongside the already found number of women (791.8 thousand), will allow for the calculation of the required difference. I will perform a targeted Google search using specific terms to find this missing piece of information.\n</plan>\n\n<action>\ntool: google_search\nparameters: {\"query\": \"number of men with tertiary education in Bulgaria 2011 census\"}\n</action>']\n[Observation 5: 'Tool 'wikipedia_knowledge_searcher' executed.\nResult: {\n  \"output\": \"Search results for 'Bulgaria 2011 census tertiary education gender statistics':\\n1. List of countries by tertiary education attainment\\n2. Demographics of Bulgaria\\n3. Education\\n4. Demographics of Croatia\\n5. Languages in censuses\\n6. Women in STEM\\n7. Demographics of Greece\\n8. Demographics of Canada\\n9. White people in the United Kingdom\\n10. Educational attainment in the United States\\n\\nExtracted text:\\nThese are lists of countries by number of people who attained tertiary education.\\nTertiary education is the educational level following the completion of a school providing a secondary education. The World Bank, for example, defines tertiary education as including universities as well as institutions that teach specific capacities of higher learning such as colleges, technical training institutes, community colleges, nursing schools, research laboratories, centers of excellence, and distance learning centers.\\n\\n\\n== National Statistics ==\\nThis is a list of the overall proportion of the population who have attained tertiary education (at least ISCED 5) in each country. Whenever possible, the proportion is calculated from only those aged 25 and above, as this largely excludes the people who are still in the process of attaining tertiary education and yields a more accurate percentage. \\n\\n\\n== 2022 OECD data ==\\nThis is a list of countries by the proportions of 25- to 64-year-olds having completed tertiary education as published by the OECD. This list includes non-OECD member countries: Argentina, Brazil, China, Costa Rica, India, Indonesia, and South Africa.\\n\\n\\n== 2019 OECD data ==\\n\\nThis list includes non-OECD member countries: Brazil, China, Costa Rica, Indonesia, Russia, Saudi Arabia, and South Africa.\\n\\n\\n== Countries by level of tertiary education ==\\nThis is a list of countries by the level of tertiary education completed by 25–64 year olds as of the year 2022.\\n\\n\\n== See also ==\\nEducation by country\\nList of universities and colleges by country\\nList of countries by secondary education attainment\\n\\n\\n== Notes ==\\n\\n\\n== References ==\\nBrick, Jean (2006). \\\"What is academic culture?\\\". Academic Culture: A Student's Guide to Studying at University. Sydney, N.S.W: National Centre for English Language Teaching and Research. pp. 1–10. ISBN 978-1-74138-135-1.\\n\\n\\n== External links ==\\nTertiary education statistics, UNESCO\\nQuality Research International – (Glossary)\"\n}', Action 5: '<memory>\nFrom the past observations and actions:\n- In previous steps, efforts were made to find the gender split data for individuals who completed tertiary education according to the 2011 Bulgarian census.\n- It was found that 791.8 thousand women completed tertiary education, but the number of men was not determined.\n- The search results have repeatedly pointed to a PDF document from the Bulgarian National Statistical Institute, which might contain the required data.\n- The attempts to extract text from this PDF resulted in corrupted or incomplete information.\n- The recent Google searches did not yield the number of men who completed tertiary education.\n\nThese memory points highlight the partial information obtained and the gap in data that remains to be filled to calculate the difference in thousands of women.\n</memory>\n\n<reflection>\nReflecting on the last action and its outcome:\n- The last action involved performing another Google search specifically for the number of men with tertiary education according to the 2011 Bulgarian census.\n- This search did not yield the desired result as the number of men was not found in the search results.\n- Although partial progress was made by finding the number of women, the task goal remains incomplete without the number of men.\n- The outcome indicates a need to explore alternative methods or sources to obtain the missing data.\n</reflection>\n\n<plan>\nThe next step is to search for a reliable source that might contain the complete gender split data from the 2011 Bulgarian census. Using the Wikipedia tool could be effective for finding a summary or article that includes these statistics, as Wikipedia often aggregates such data. The goal is to find the number of men alongside the already known number of women to complete the calculation.\n</plan>\n\n<action>\ntool: wikipedia_knowledge_searcher\nparameters: {\"query\": \"Bulgaria 2011 census tertiary education gender statistics\"}\n</action>']\n[Observation 6: 'Tool 'google_search' executed.\nResult: [\n  {\n    \"title\": \"2011 POPULATION CENSUS – Main Results\",\n    \"link\": \"https://www.nsi.bg/census2011/pdocs2/census2011final_en.pdf\",\n    \"snippet\": \"Feb 1, 2011 ... Among the Bulgarian citizens who have stayed abroad persons with secondary education are prevailing - 45.8%, with tertiary - 34.1%, with 